as; Gore and Elk Mountains/Central Mountain Valleys; West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Flat Tops","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.af55203ae8b090e7462a743aa605f50df31e2dff.003.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.af55203ae8b090e7462a743aa605f50df31e2dff.003.1"},{"event":"Flood Advisory","headline":"Flood Advisory issued November 2 at 9:17AM MST until November 3 at 9:15AM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall is expected.\n\n* WHERE...Southwestern Mesa County in west central Colorado...\nWest Central Montrose County in west central Colorado...\nSoutheastern Grand County in east central Utah...\n\n* WHEN...Until 915 AM MST Tuesday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Minor flooding in low-lying and poor drainage areas.\nWater over roadways. Some low-water crossings may become\nimpassable. River or stream flows are elevated.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 915 AM MST, gauge reports indicated high water levels due to\nextended rainfall and runoff. Rapid river rises will cause minor\nflooding in Dolores River.\n- Dolores River is the most likely place to experience minor\nflooding.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nGateway.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-03T09:15:00-07:00","severity":"Minor","areaDesc":"Mesa, CO; Montrose, CO; Grand, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.018a680203b7eff0fdf443da12b5f90a1801752b.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.018a680203b7eff0fdf443da12b5f90a1801752b.001.1"},{"event":"Avalanche Advisory","headline":"Avalanche Advisory issued October 31 at 7:16PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"SABCO\n\nTHE FOLLOWING MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at\nwww.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\nBeyer & Pritchett","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-11-02T19:56:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 021956 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 1256 PM MST Mon Nov 2 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Rivers across southwest CO and southeast UT remain rain swollen due to runoff. The San Juan River at Pagosa Springs remains in major flood stage. - Light rain below 10000 feet will continue, with snow above. Winter weather highlights continue for the mount","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-11-02T18:59:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... This is an automatically generated forecast with no discussion. The normal Fire Weather Forecast will resume in its entirety in the spring. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS CAN BE EXPECTED NEAR ANY SHOWERS OR THUNDERSTORMS... ...THE SMOKE DATA IS CALCULATED FROM AN AVERAGE AT 6000 FEET MSL... COZ200-022300- Little Snake Forecast Area- 1159 AM MST Mon Nov 2 2026","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":{"station_id":"oth01","station_name":"Olathe","distance